  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates photocatalytic water splitting driven by sunlight. On the left, a suspension of catalyst particles in water is exposed to light, and the products hydrogen and oxygen are shown evolving from the liquid, conveying the overall goal of converting water into fuel and oxygen gas.\n\nThe enlarged schematic on the right explains the mechanism on a single particle. A semiconductor photocatalyst absorbs light with energy greater than its band gap, generating separated charge carriers, shown as an electron–hole pair. These carriers must move to different sites on the particle surface in a sequence of steps. One side is decorated with an oxidation co-catalyst, where water is oxidized to oxygen and protons. The other side carries a reduction co-catalyst, where protons are reduced to hydrogen. Dashed arrows inside the particle indicate a competing recombination pathway, emphasizing that charge loss can occur before the carriers reach the reactive surface sites.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights that efficient solar water splitting depends on three linked processes: light absorption and charge generation, charge separation and transport to the surface, and catalytic surface reactions that produce O2 and H2.",
